From af33419201a70f915a49559361351e89999e6414 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 19:54:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] headerv2: index a BLAKE2b chain end to end

Wires HeaderV2 and AnyHeader through the parts of electrs that touch a
header: chain, types, db, index, p2p, status, tracker, electrum. Before
this, the type existed but nothing used it.

Verified against a real BLAKE2b chain, not only unit tests. Against a
Bitcoin Knots v29.4.1.knots20260508rc2 regtest node with activation at
height 20, electrs indexed all 132 blocks (19 v1, 113 v2), reached the
node's own bestblockhash, and then agreed with the node on:

  - every transaction at every height, via transaction.id_from_pos
  - scripthash history, balance and utxos for addresses paid in v2
    blocks, to the satoshi
  - merkle proofs that recompute to each header's own merkle root

Three things worth calling out.

**bitcoin_slices was the real obstacle, not rust-bitcoin.** Every block
electrs parses goes through bsl::Block::visit, which reads the
transaction-count varint from a hardcoded offset 80. On a 164-byte
header that offset lands inside m_nonce2. It does not reliably error: on
the fixture block it reads a count of zero, returns Ok, and visits
nothing, so a four-transaction block indexes as empty and nothing
anywhere reports a problem. Rather than fork the crate, visit_block_txs
reimplements the eight lines of Block::visit that matter using its
public scan_len and Transaction; the transaction parsing, which is all
of the cost, is still theirs and unchanged.

**The v2 payload in AnyHeader is boxed.** Chain holds one header per
block for the whole chain, so this type's size is multiplied by height.
Inline, the enum is 168 bytes and a 900k-block v1 chain would go from
about 100 MB resident to 180 MB for no benefit, since every header on
such a chain is v1. Boxed, it is 88 bytes and only v2 headers pay an
allocation. A test asserts the size so this cannot be undone by
accident. The cost is that AnyHeader is Clone, not Copy.

**Stored rows are decoded by length, network bytes by the version bit.**
AnyHeader::parse reads bit 31 exactly as Knots does (CBlockHeader's
SERIALIZE_METHODS branches on it with no activation-height gate), so on
the wire this code stands where consensus stands. But rows read back out
of our own database use parse_exact, which trusts the row's length,
because we wrote it. That keeps stored data readable regardless of
whether some historical miner ever set the top version bit. Header rows
are RocksDB keys, which are already variable-length, so the storage
format needed no change beyond letting the length vary; iter_headers
gained its own iterator since DBIterator<N> filters on a fixed key size,
which was also what kept the one-byte TIP_KEY out of the results.

Missed on the first pass: filter_block_txs_outputs, filter_block_txs_inputs
and lookup_transaction also called bsl::Block::visit. The substitution
skipped them because they name no header type at all. Unit tests did not
catch it; the end-to-end run did, as an empty scripthash.get_history for
an address that had been paid. There is now a status.rs test on a v2
fixture block that fails if that walker comes back, confirmed by
reverting the fix.

Fixtures in src/tests/blake2b_regtest.json are a regtest chain crossing
the activation: headers 1..24, and whole blocks at 19, 20 and 131. The
gitignore negation widened from one file to src/tests/*.json.

fmt clean, clippy clean, 32 tests passing, up from 30.
